;;; A rule's `:assert` splits a name that starts with `neq` —
;;; regression `kw-neq-in-assert` (`corpus/regressions.toml`): a reserved word is
;;; a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The witness the family was found by: one fact, `(p a)`; a relation `neqw`;
;;; one rule, `r`, concluding `(neqw ?x)` from `(p ?x)`; and a query, `(neqw
;;; ?y)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `neqw` is a name like any other. `r`
;;; concludes `(neqw a)`, so root holds `(neqw a)` and `(p a)`, and the blind
;;; enumerator has nothing left to propose over the program's names: H is
;;; empty and R is {∅}. The query's one class is `?y = a`. It is the answer of
;;; `kw-neq-in-assert-twin.ein`, this file with `neqw` renamed `nneqw`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read `(neqw ?x)` as the keyword
;;; `neq` followed by `w`, so `r` concluded `(neq w a)`, a fact of the reserved
;;; `neq`. `w` became a name of the program, H was `(neqw a)`, `(neqw w)` and
;;; `(p w)`, and R was their eight subsets. The query printed as `(neq w ?y)`,
;;; *the goal holds: it has no variable*. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`,
;;; release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) A keyword is a
;;; whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1 states (Q-M1e2.17), and the
;;; program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation neqw Node :why "neqw holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (neqw ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so neqw holds of {?x}")
(query :goal (neqw ?y))
